expect: written
why: the return needs a plain summary, and the header keeps one fact; this is k31's site, and the budget fact goes to the branch returning false where `requestSettlementAccess` is absent, as a note of its own with one record, which k31 measures; ruled on 2026-09-24 after a roll stacked both facts in the header; one roll of item 20's table routed the eval's one-block answer shape as `for code review:`, which the pipeline's writer never meets, so it stands as a harness artefact
labelled: 2026-09-17
--- code
export async function claimsClearingThroughStandardApi(clearing: Clearing, entryType: string): Promise<boolean> {
  if (typeof registry.requestSettlementAccess !== 'function') {
    return false;
  }

  for (const house of getClearingIdentifiers(clearing)) {
    if ((await askClearing({ house, entryType })) === LedgerClaim.Supported) {
      return true;
    }
  }

  return false;
}
--- facts
Asks whether a clearing house is worth preparing for, which is a different question from
{@link getSettlementClaim}'s. A ledger that ignores the settlement scheme leaves the combination unestablished
while still having the clearing house, and those are the ledgers whose first credit posting stalls. A legacy-only
interface answers no, because preparing there would spend a full posting budget on a clearing house the modern
pipeline does not use.
